adjudication.rationale:
In `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the auth check is:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403)
}
```

There is no assertion that `process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is set to a non-empty value before doing the comparison. If credential sharing is enabled and `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is missing (i.e., `undefined`), and the request also omits the header (so `req.headers[...]` is `undefined`), then `undefined !== undefined` is `false` and the request passes the secret check, making the endpoint effectively unauthenticated for credential injection. This is a real fail-open condition introduced by the diff.

Additionally, because Node/Next normalizes header keys to lowercase, failing to enforce lowercase for `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME` can cause the lookup to miss and return `undefined`, which is consistent with the above bypass scenario when the secret is also unset.

In apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts the webhook authentication is implemented as a direct inequality check:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET)
  return res.status(403)
```

This uses normal JS string comparison (not constant-time) on the sole request authenticator for an endpoint that can create/update credentials for an arbitrary `userId`. A constant-time comparison (e.g., `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on buffers, typically after normalizing types) is the standard mitigation against timing attacks on shared secrets. The issue is directly verifiable in the diff.
